---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [["Can I have SPF without DKIM?", "Yes, but DMARC alignment will fail whenever the mail is forwarded or the sending IP is not in your SPF record. DKIM provides forward-safe authentication."], ["Can I have DKIM without SPF?", "Yes, but SPF provides IP-level authorization that DKIM does not. DKIM alone cannot prevent IP spoofing if the signing key is compromised."], ["Which is more important for deliverability?", "Both matter. Gmail and Microsoft require both SPF and DKIM to pass for bulk senders. DKIM is slightly more critical because it survives forwarding."]];

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: "SPF vs DKIM",
  description: "SPF vs DKIM: SPF authorizes sending IPs via DNS TXT, DKIM cryptographically signs messages. You need both for DMARC.",
  inLanguage: 'en',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://promptdns.com/favicon.svg' } },
  datePublished: '2026-10-27',
  dateModified: '2026-10-27',
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map(([q, a]) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Email Authentication', item: 'https://promptdns.com/email-authentication/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: "SPF vs DKIM" },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SPF vs DKIM: Key Differences, When to Use Each, and Why You Need Both"
  description="SPF vs DKIM: SPF authorizes sending IPs via DNS TXT, DKIM cryptographically signs messages. You need both for DMARC."
  schema={[article, faqSchema, breadcrumbs]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SPF vs DKIM</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SPF vs DKIM</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-dim">SPF vs DKIM compared: SPF authorizes sending IPs via DNS, DKIM cryptographically signs messages. You need both for DMARC.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class='pdns-table' style='width:100%'><thead><tr><th class='whitespace-nowrap'></th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>SPF</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>DKIM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class='pd-mono'>RFC</td><td>7208</td><td>6376</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>What it authenticates</td><td>Envelope sender (MAIL FROM)</td><td>Message content (From header)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>How it works</td><td>IP-based: checks sending IP against DNS TXT record</td><td>Cryptographic: verifies digital signature in email header</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Survives forwarding</td><td>No (forwarding changes the IP)</td><td>Yes (signature travels with the message)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>DNS record</td><td>TXT at root: v=spf1 ...</td><td>TXT at selector._domainkey: v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=...</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>DNS lookups</td><td>Up to 10 per evaluation</td><td>1 (to fetch the public key)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Can be broken by</td><td>New sending IP not in the record</td><td>Key rotation without DNS update</td></tr></tbody></table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      <h2>Technical details</h2>
      <p class='mt-3'>SPF and DKIM are complementary email authentication protocols: SPF (RFC 7208) authorizes sending IP addresses via a DNS TXT record, while DKIM (RFC 6376) cryptographically signs message content with a private key.</p><p class='mt-3'>DMARC requires at least one of SPF or DKIM to pass AND align with the visible From header domain; using both provides redundancy when one mechanism fails.</p><p class='mt-3'>SPF breaks when email is forwarded (the forwarding server's IP is not in the original SPF record), while DKIM survives forwarding because the cryptographic signature travels with the message content.</p>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">The biggest deliverability gap I see is domains that have SPF but no DKIM, because SPF alone breaks on any forwarding scenario (mailing lists, auto-forwarders, CRM outbound). Always set up both: SPF as the baseline, DKIM as the forward-safe layer.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I have SPF without DKIM?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, but DMARC alignment will fail whenever the mail is forwarded or the sending IP is not in your SPF record. DKIM provides forward-safe authentication.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I have DKIM without SPF?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, but SPF provides IP-level authorization that DKIM does not. DKIM alone cannot prevent IP spoofing if the signing key is compromised.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which is more important for deliverability?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Both matter. Gmail and Microsoft require both SPF and DKIM to pass for bulk senders. DKIM is slightly more critical because it survives forwarding.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Check your domain with our free tools:
        <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF checker</a>,
        <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC checker</a>,
        <a href="/dkim-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DKIM checker</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
